/*  Functions as values: a price built by applying a list of adjustments in order.

    A lambda is a function written where a value goes — no name, the return type inferred from
    the body, and always carrying the variables it captured. Everything here is a function being
    treated as data: made, captured, stored in a series, passed, and returned.

    ★★ THE ONE THAT MATTERS: `surcharge-of` is a function that RETURNS a function, and the lambda
    it hands back captures `rate` — the factory's own parameter, which is gone by the time anyone
    calls the result. Two closures made from the same factory must keep DIFFERENT captured rates,
    which is what the `service only` and `handling only` lines below prove: same code, same input,
    two answers. A closure implementation that shared one environment would print the same number
    twice and still look like it worked.

    ★ `add-fee` captures a top-level binding instead of a parameter — the other half of the
    capture rule, and the cheaper one to get right.

    ★ A series whose element type is a FUNCTION type is spelled with the element type written out
    in full: `a series of number function given (the number)`. ⚠ Where the name sits is what
    separates "a series of functions" from "a function taking a series" — see REFERENCE, which
    records that sharp edge.

    ⚠ A suspension may not appear inside a lambda: a named body becomes a factory handing back a
    closure, and a lambda already IS one. Give the body a name if it needs to `bury`.
*/

Bind number function given (the number) to surcharge-of, given (the number rate):
    Return a function given (the number amount):
        Return amount + (amount * rate / 100).
    Done.
Done.

Bind number to apply-all, given (the series of number function given (the number) steps,
                                 the number opening):
    Define running as opening.
    For each step in steps, repeat:
        The running becomes cast step on (running).
    Done.
    Return running.
Done.

Bind number to apply-to, given (the number amount, the number function step given (the number)):
    Return cast step on (amount).
Done.

Define flat-fee as 9.
Define add-fee as a function given (the number amount): Return amount + flat-fee. Done.

Define service as cast surcharge-of on (10).
Define handling as cast surcharge-of on (5).

State "service only: {cast service on (200)}".
State "handling only: {cast handling on (200)}".

Define pricing as a series of number function given (the number) with (service, handling, add-fee).
State "all three in order: {cast apply-all on (pricing, 200)}".

State "then a flat discount: {cast apply-to on (240, a function given (the number charge): Return charge - 40. Done)}".
